# Use clear language

Plain English is the standard. Specialists prefer it too, because they can read it faster.

<h2 id="wg-010">Use familiar, concrete words</h2>

Use the shortest word that keeps the technical meaning. Keep a technical term when it is the exact label, and explain it the first time.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| The actuator is de-energised. | The socket reports that it is off. This does not confirm that the appliance is safe to work on. |
| Initiate the pairing procedure. | Start pairing. |
| An entity is exposed. | An entity is a control or reading in Home Assistant. This lamp is one entity. |

Do not swap a precise term for a comforting one that is wrong.

<h2 id="wg-013">Make sentences easy to follow</h2>

One main idea per sentence. Use the active voice. Use the passive when the thing that happened matters more than who did it: “The schedule was not saved.” Put a condition before the action it limits.

Check any sentence over 20 words. This is a prompt, not a limit. Do not cut a device name or a condition to hit a number.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| The schedule is unable to be saved at this time as a result of the connection to Home Assistant having been interrupted. | Home Assistant could not save the schedule. The connection dropped. |
| Heating will be turned off unless the window sensor reports Closed and the target is above 16°C. | If the window sensor reports Closed and the target is above 16°C, the heating stays on. Otherwise it turns off. |

“Schedule not saved” is fine in a short result message when the next sentence says what happened.

<h2 id="wg-014">Write important conditions in full</h2>

Use “cannot” and “do not”, not “can’t” and “don’t”, in conditions, warnings and short instructions. Elsewhere “you’ll” and “it’s” are fine. Do not mix “cannot” and “can’t” on one screen.

Expanded forms are easier to translate into any language and harder to misread at a glance. Do not change contractions in native translations, quotations or names.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| You can’t start it if remote’s off. | You cannot start the washing machine here while remote control is off. |
| Don’t unplug the hub. | Do not unplug the hub during the update. |

Avoid idioms such as “give it a whirl” and “in a pinch”.

Use the exact Home Assistant name when you tell someone where to find a control, and a short explanation when you tell them what it means. For a single Home Assistant word, look it up in [A to Z Technical](../style-guides/technical-a-to-z.html).

<h2 id="wg-020">Use one word for one operation</h2>

Use the same word for the same operation. Use a different word for a different operation.

<%= example('wg-household-terms') %>

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Remove the automation (when you mean delete it) | Delete automation |
| Disable the automation (when you mean turn off future starts) | Turn off automation |
| Remove from this dashboard (when it also deletes the device) | Remove from this dashboard. The device stays in Home Assistant. |

<h2 id="wg-021">Keep native labels and translations</h2>

When you point someone to a screen, use the label Home Assistant shows, in the language it shows. Explain an unfamiliar term next to it.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Open Messages. (the screen says Notifications) | Open **Notifications**. This shows messages from your automations. |
| Find the identification of the light. | Find the **Entity ID** of the light. |

<h2 id="wg-022">Keep names exactly as people typed them</h2>

Do not change the case, spelling, punctuation or language of a name someone gave. Do not guess a relationship from it. Do not change an ID when someone changes a name.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Nain’s lamp → Nain’s Lamp | Nain’s lamp |
| Mam’s heater → Mother’s heater | Mam’s heater |

<h2 id="wg-025">Keep literal values literal</h2>

Put an entity ID, a key, a path or a version in code formatting. Do not change underscores, dashes or quotation marks inside it.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Enter the reading lamp ID: light.reading-lamp. | Entity ID: `light.reading_lamp` |
| Set mode to "heat". (curly quotes copied into YAML) | Set `mode: heat` |

Label an example ID as an example. Never ask someone to paste a password or token into a field that does not need one.

<h2 id="wg-060">Do not assume who is reading</h2>

Do not assume a relationship, ownership, ability or who is at home.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Ask your husband to check the fuse box in your home. | Ask someone who can help with the device, if you need support. |
| Your landlord must fix this. | Check your tenancy agreement or contact the owner of the property. |

Where a maker’s manual gives a safety instruction, link to it or quote it. Do not paraphrase it. Do not write your own safety advice.

<h2 id="wg-062">Use respectful, specific language</h2>

Describe what happens, not what a person is like. Avoid labels such as “normal user” and words like “simply”, “obviously” and “just”.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Normal users can ignore this setting. | Most households do not need to change this setting. |
| Simply restart the router. | Turn the router off, wait 30 seconds, then turn it on again. |

<h2 id="wg-063">Do not assume one way to see, hear or act</h2>

Do not rely on colour, sound, position or a gesture alone. Say it in words too.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Tap the green button on the right. | Select **Turn on**. |
| Listen for the beep. | The light flashes twice and the hub beeps. If you cannot see or hear it, check **Status** in the app. |

<h2 id="wg-065">Preserve language and translation</h2>

Write English in English and Welsh in Welsh. Do not machine-translate a sentence and publish it unchecked, and do not mix languages in one control.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Lamp (lamp Cymraeg: lamp) | English: Reading lamp. Welsh: Lamp darllen. Both written by a person who speaks the language. |
| Translate the page into Welsh automatically. | Ask a Welsh speaker to write the Welsh version. Publish the two versions together. |

A household may name devices in any language. Leave those names as typed.
